{{nihongo|'''Ishiwara Prefecture'''|石原県|Ishiwara Ken}} is a volcanic, heavily forested [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located on the southern coast of [[Harujima]] Island. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 925,723. It is the largest prefecture by land area with an area of 6,395.71 square kilometres, yet has one of the lowest population densities in the nation. Its capital city is [[Ishiwara, Ishiwara|Ishiwara]]. Ishiwara Prefecture is renowned as Okaiken's primary source of high-quality timber, celebrated for its mineral-rich volcanic soils that produce exceptionally fast-growing trees, and its historical shipbuilding heritage dating to the Murakami naval era. The prefecture is often referred to as the {{nihongo|"Forest Forge of Harujima"|春島の森の炉|Harujima no Mori no Ro}}.

==History==
[[File:Ishiwara Volcano Eruption 1783.jpg|thumb|left|250px|An Edo-period depiction of the 1783 eruption of Mount Ishiwara, which devastated the central basin but enriched the surrounding soils with volcanic ash.]]

===The Murakami Naval Domain===
Prior to the [[Saito Restoration]], the volcanic coastal region now known as Ishiwara was granted to [[Murakami Motosuke]] on March 8, 1590, by [[Honda Shigetada]] in recognition of his service to the Honda clan and his role in preserving the Anjō-Murakami Suigun naval fleet following the death of [[Anjō Motonobu]]. The domain was considered undesirable by other lords—its volcanic terrain and frequent eruptions made traditional rice cultivation nearly impossible, and its dense forests were viewed as an obstacle rather than an asset.

Motosuke, however, recognized what others had overlooked. The same geography that precluded agriculture made Ishiwara ideal for shipbuilding. Under his direction, the domain developed extensive shipyards, rope works, and dry docks that would become the primary forward base of the Anjō-Murakami Suigun. The {{nihongo|Ishiwara Shipyards|石原造船所|Ishiwara ZōSENSHO}} rivaled the royal naval yards near Okami, producing sekibune warships and flat-bottomed transport vessels that would prove essential to Honda naval supremacy.

===The Timber Transformation===
[[File:Ishiwara Logging 1850.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Loggers felling massive cedar trees in the Ishiwara highlands during the late Edo period.]]

Following Motosuke's death in 1632, his successors continued developing the domain's timber resources. The volcanic soils of Ishiwara—enriched by centuries of ash deposits—produced trees that grew at nearly twice the rate of those in non-volcanic regions. The {{nihongo|'''Ishiwara Cedar'''|石原杉|Ishiwara Sugi}} became legendary among shipbuilders for its exceptional strength-to-weight ratio, a quality attributed to the rapid growth patterns induced by mineral-rich soils.

By the late Edo period, Ishiwara had transformed from a naval base into Okaiken's primary timber supplier. The Honda Shogunate established the {{nihongo|'''Forest Bureau'''|山林局|Sanrin-kyoku}} in 1785 to manage the sustainable harvest of Ishiwara's forests—the first such conservation agency in the nation. The bureau implemented rotational logging practices that ensured continuous timber production while preventing deforestation, a system that remains in modified form today.

===The Mining Boom===
The 19th century brought the discovery of extensive mineral deposits in the volcanic formations surrounding Mount Ishiwara. Sulfur, essential for gunpowder production and agricultural fertilizers, was discovered in commercial quantities in 1842. The {{nihongo|'''Alps-Toge Sulfur Mines'''|アルプス峠硫黄鉱山|Arupusu-Tōge Iō Kōzan}} became one of the largest sulfur operations in Harujima, attracting workers from across the prefecture and spurring the development of mining communities in the highland regions.

The mining industry transformed Ishiwara's economy, providing employment for residents who could not work in the dangerous logging operations or the limited agricultural sector. Towns like [[Alps-Toge]] and [[Kita-Bunki]] grew around the mining infrastructure, and the prefecture developed a dual identity as both the nation's timber forge and its mineral storehouse.

===Modern Development===
[[File:Ishiwara Reforestation 1965.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Reforestation efforts in the 1960s, part of the prefecture's sustainable forestry management program.]]

The post-[[Great Adonian War]] era brought significant changes to Ishiwara. The completion of the coastal highway in 1958 connected the prefecture to the Okami metropolitan region, enabling rapid transport of timber and minerals to urban markets. The establishment of the {{nihongo|'''Ishiwara Forestry Research Institute'''|石原林業研究所|Ishiwara Ringyō Kenkyūjo}} in 1962 pioneered sustainable logging practices and developed faster-growing tree cultivars specifically adapted to volcanic soils.

The prefecture also capitalized on its unique agricultural potential. The volcanic soils, rich in minerals and exceptionally well-drained, proved ideal for cultivating certain fruits that thrived in the nutrient-dense earth. The {{nihongo|'''Ishiwara Peach'''|石原桃|Ishiwara Momo}} and {{nihongo|'''Volcano Pear'''|火山梨|Kazan Nashi}} became premium agricultural products, commanding high prices in metropolitan markets for their intense sweetness and distinctive mineral notes.

Today, Ishiwara Prefecture balances its extractive industries with environmental conservation, maintaining the largest protected forest reserves in Okaiken while continuing to supply the nation's construction and manufacturing sectors with high-quality timber.

==Geography==
[[File:Mount Ishiwara Summit.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The summit of Mount Ishiwara, the active volcano that dominates the prefecture's central highlands.]]

Ishiwara Prefecture occupies a vast volcanic region on the southern coast of Harujima Island. The prefecture's defining geographic feature is {{nihongo|'''Mount Ishiwara'''|石原岳|Ishiwara-dake}}, an active stratovolcano rising to 2,847 meters above sea level. The volcano's frequent eruptions—averaging one significant event every 40-50 years—have shaped both the prefecture's topography and its economic development, depositing layers of mineral-rich ash that create exceptionally fertile soils for rapid tree growth.

The volcanic activity has created a landscape of dramatic contrasts: rugged lava flows and ash plains in the immediate vicinity of the crater, transitioning to dense primary forests on the lower slopes, and finally to coastal plains and numerous offshore islands. The {{nihongo|'''Ishiwara Caldera'''|石原カルデラ|Ishiwara Karudera}}, a massive collapsed volcanic crater measuring 12 kilometers in diameter, forms the prefecture's central basin and contains the capital city.

===Volcanic Hazards and Population Distribution===
The constant threat of volcanic activity has profoundly influenced settlement patterns. The majority of the population is concentrated in coastal areas and on offshore islands, away from the most dangerous eruption zones. The cities of [[Kitamisaki]] and [[Minamimisaki]] on the northern and southern peninsulas, respectively, have grown into significant population centers precisely because of their distance from the volcano's immediate danger radius.

The central caldera region, despite its fertile soils, maintains a relatively low population density due to evacuation planning requirements. The prefecture maintains an extensive early warning system and has constructed numerous evacuation shelters and escape routes, making Ishiwara a model for volcanic disaster preparedness.

