==Economy==
[[File:Ishiwara Logging Operation.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A modern logging operation in the Ishiwara highlands, using sustainable selective harvesting techniques.]]

Ishiwara Prefecture possesses a resource-extraction economy centered on forestry, mining, and specialized agriculture, supplemented by fishing in the coastal waters. Unlike the manufacturing and service-oriented prefectures of the metropolitan corridor, Ishiwara has maintained its identity as Okaiken's primary supplier of raw materials—timber, minerals, and specialty agricultural products.

===Forestry and Timber===
The forestry sector is the cultural and economic foundation of Ishiwara Prefecture. The volcanic soils produce trees that grow 40-60% faster than those in non-volcanic regions, enabling shorter harvest rotations and higher sustainable yields. The prefecture's forests cover approximately 78% of its land area, representing the largest contiguous forest reserve in Okaiken.

The {{nihongo|'''Ishiwara Cedar'''|石原杉|Ishiwara Sugi}} is the prefecture's signature timber product, prized throughout Okaiken for its straight grain, natural resistance to humidity and insects, and exceptional workability. The rapid growth induced by volcanic soils produces wood with a distinctive tight grain pattern that structural engineers favor for both construction and fine woodworking.

The forestry industry operates under the nation's oldest sustainable management system, established by the Edo-period Forest Bureau and modernized by the Ishiwara Forestry Research Institute. Selective harvesting techniques ensure continuous forest cover, while the volcanic soils' natural fertility reduces the need for artificial fertilization in replanting operations. The industry directly employs approximately 35,000 workers in logging, processing, and transport operations.

===Mining and Minerals===
[[File:Alps-Toge Sulfur Mine.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The historic Alps-Toge sulfur mine, still in operation after more than 150 years of continuous extraction.]]

The volcanic formations of Ishiwara contain extensive mineral deposits, particularly sulfur, pumice, and various construction aggregates. The {{nihongo|'''Alps-Toge Sulfur Mines'''|アルプス峠硫黄鉱山|Arupusu-Tōge Iō Kōzan}} remain one of the largest sulfur operations in Harujima, producing industrial-grade sulfur for chemical manufacturing and agricultural applications.

Pumice mining has grown significantly in recent decades, as the lightweight volcanic stone has found applications in construction, horticulture, and water filtration. The {{nihongo|'''Ishiwara Pumice'''|石原軽石|Ishiwara Keiseki}} is exported throughout the Shendan Ocean region for use in lightweight concrete and soil amendment.

The mining sector employs approximately 12,000 workers and contributes significantly to the prefecture's export economy. Environmental regulations require extensive land reclamation following mining operations, and many former mine sites have been converted to fruit orchards or reforested.

===Volcanic Agriculture===
The mineral-rich volcanic soils support a specialized agricultural sector focused on high-value fruits that benefit from the unique growing conditions. The rapid drainage and exceptional mineral content of volcanic earth produce fruits with intense flavors and distinctive characteristics.

The {{nihongo|'''Ishiwara Peach'''|石原桃|Ishiwara Momo}} is the prefecture's premier agricultural product, known for its exceptional sweetness and subtle mineral undertones attributed to the volcanic soil composition. The {{nihongo|'''Volcano Pear'''|火山梨|Kazan Nashi}}, cultivated in the caldera region, similarly commands premium prices in metropolitan markets for its crisp texture and complex flavor profile.

Agricultural production is concentrated in the caldera basin and coastal plains, where volcanic ash deposits have created exceptionally fertile soils. The industry employs approximately 18,000 workers and has spawned a growing agritourism sector, with visitors drawn to orchard tours and fruit-picking experiences.

===Fishing===
The coastal waters surrounding Ishiwara support a traditional fishing industry, particularly in the offshore islands and peninsula communities. The {{nihongo|'''Kitamisaki Fishing Fleet'''|北岬漁船団|Kitamisaki Gyosendan}} operates from the northern peninsula, harvesting horse mackerel, sea bream, and various shellfish from the rich fishing grounds of the Shendan Ocean.

The fishing sector, while smaller than forestry or mining, provides essential employment for island communities and contributes to the prefecture's food self-sufficiency. Approximately 8,000 workers are employed in fishing and seafood processing operations.
Beyond its commercial forestry reserves, Ishiwara has emerged as a cornerstone of Okaiken's mineral sovereignty through the development of supercritical geothermal extraction. Anchored in the volcanic hot-spring zones, Ishiwara Brine Technologies operates closed-loop direct lithium extraction plants that process hydrothermal fluids pumped from three kilometers beneath the surface. Using proprietary ion-exchange adsorption columns, the facility extracts thousands of tons of battery-grade lithium carbonate, boron, and rare alkali metals annually before reinjecting the cooled mineralized brines back into deep aquifers, completely avoiding the environmental scarring of open-pit mining while supplying vital battery feedstocks to Yonezawa Motors.

==Transportation==
[[File:Ishiwara Coastal Highway.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Ishiwara Coastal Highway winding along the southern shore, with the volcanic peaks visible in the distance.]]

Ishiwara Prefecture maintains transportation infrastructure designed to accommodate both the extractive industries' heavy freight requirements and the unique challenges of volcanic disaster preparedness.

===Road===
The '''Ishiwara Coastal Highway''' runs along the southern shore, connecting the prefecture to the Okami metropolitan region to the west and to neighboring prefectures to the east. The highway is engineered with multiple evacuation routes and reinforced bridges designed to withstand volcanic earthquakes and ash accumulation.

The '''Caldera Ring Road''' circles the volcanic basin, providing access to the forestry operations and agricultural areas while serving as a primary evacuation route in the event of eruptions. The road features numerous emergency shelters and ash-clearing equipment stations.

===Rail===
Conventional [[Okaiken Railways]] services connect Ishiwara to the regional rail network, with the primary line running along the coast. The '''Ishiwara Line''' connects the capital to [[Kitamisaki]] and [[Minamimisaki]], providing both passenger service and freight transport for timber and mineral exports.

===Air===
[[Ishiwara Airport]] provides domestic connections to major cities, with daily flights to Okami and seasonal services to other destinations. The airport is equipped with specialized volcanic ash filtration systems and serves as a secondary evacuation hub during eruption emergencies.

===Maritime===
The prefecture's numerous offshore islands are connected by ferry services operating from ports in Ishiwara city, Kitamisaki, and Minamimisaki. The ferry network provides essential transportation for island residents and supports the fishing industry's distribution network.

